title: Why the two query fields are AND-ed
tags: [[metarecord-list panel]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: The metarecord list's simplified query and normal DSL used to be one query in two forms, the normal DSL mirroring the other or, frozen, replacing it; since October 2026 they are two filters AND-ed, so adding one never means giving up the other.

Until October 2026 the [[metarecord-list panel]] had one query in two forms. The
normal DSL field showed the simplified query's expansion, read-only; checking
''Freeze'' made it editable, and from then on it alone ran, whatever the simplified
field held. Unfreezing brought the expansion back and discarded the hand edit.

That made every second filter a choice between the two languages. A query written
by a command — a folder (`metarecord-list:folder`), what points at a node
(`path-ref:list-refs`), the orphans — lands in the normal DSL, frozen. Narrowing it
with a tag meant writing the tag in the normal DSL, since typing it in the simplified
field unfroze the normal one and dropped the folder. And the focus and clear commands
had to freeze or unfreeze as they went, so that the field the key landed in was one
the query read.

Now the two are separate filters, AND-ed: `(<expansion>) AND (<normal DSL>)`, each
empty one adding nothing. The expansion keeps a line of its own under the simplified
field — read-only, copyable, hidden by an arrow — so what the simplified query means
is still in view. There is no Freeze and no state that decides which field runs:
both always do.

What is given up: a hand edit of the expansion no longer replaces the simplified
query by itself. It is copied into the normal DSL and the simplified field emptied —
two steps where the box was one.

A command that writes a query chooses, by its last argument, whether its query is the
whole query (`replace`, the default: the simplified field is emptied, as the old
freeze ignored it) or AND-ed with the simplified one (`and`). Replacing stays the
default because a key like <<key "metarecord-list:folder" "g f">> means "show me this
folder", and an old, unrelated simplified query silently narrowing it would look like
missing files.

A saved workspace of version 2 is converted as it is read (version 3,
[[Saved workspaces]]): frozen, its simplified query is emptied, since it did not run;
not frozen, its normal DSL, which only held the expansion.
